HARD CONSTRAINTS
1. No arbitrary-URL fetching. Map-link support is limited to an allowlist (google.com/maps, maps.google.com, maps.app.goo.gl, goo.gl/maps). Parse coordinates from the URL text where possible; for short links, follow redirects only on allowlisted hosts, HTTPS only, max 3 redirects, short timeout, no private/loopback IP targets (SSRF protection). Unsupported links get a clear message.
2. Never claim exact precision when the source only supports a town or approximate feature. Label results: "verified" (from places database), "approximate" (geocoder), or "search only".
3. Never log or display API keys, raw provider payloads, or visitor question text.
4. Do not add heavy dependencies without a clear reason; if you add one, update requirements.txt and explain why. Keep the server runnable with `python .\chatbot.py`.
5. Respect crawler rules (HTTPS, same host, robots.txt, delay). Do not run the crawler as part of this task.
6. Keep answers grounded: factual answers must come from retrieved corpus text; if there is no evidence, say so explicitly.
7. Support English, Urdu script, and Roman Urdu input. Reply in the user's language.

WORK PHASE BY PHASE. After each phase: run all tests, summarize changed files, and stop for my confirmation before starting the next phase.

PHASE 1: Location correctness
a) Create data/places.json: verified places database. Schema per entry: id, name_en, name_ur, aliases (list incl. Roman Urdu and spelling variants), lat, lng, district, category, source_url, verified (bool), note. Seed with 10 well-known Punjab tourism sites as placeholders marked verified=false where coordinates are not confirmed; never invent coordinates silently.
b) Lookup order: places.json (alias/fuzzy match) first, then Photon.
c) Photon: request up to 5 candidates, bias/filter to Pakistan (countrycode/bbox), return city/district/country per candidate, apply a simple confidence rule, and ask the user to choose when ambiguous.
d) Replace the fixed department marker label with "approximate building location" until verified.
e) Add timeouts, clear error states, and graceful UI messages for geocoder failure.
f) Tests: ambiguous names, non-Punjab results, no result, provider error, Urdu-script label, alias match.

PHASE 2: Intent routing and parsing
a) Add an intent router: info, location, directions, image, smalltalk. Rule-based first; keep it deterministic and testable.
b) Replace the heuristic place-name stripping with an alias-aware extractor that preserves user spelling and region context.
c) Add tests for English, Urdu, and Roman Urdu phrasing, follow-up questions, and link inputs.

PHASE 3: Safe map-link input
a) Implement the allowlisted Google Maps URL handling from the constraints above.
b) Distinguish "coordinates parsed from link" from "geocoded search result" in the response.
c) Tests: valid forms, short link, non-allowlisted host, private-IP redirect, malformed URL, too many redirects.

PHASE 4: Reliability and degraded mode
a) Clear UI status when the answer came from local fallback or AI is unavailable/quota-limited (Gemini 429).
b) Provider chain: Gemini, then Groq (if configured), then local answer. Add a small in-memory response cache (bounded size, TTL) keyed by normalized question plus retrieved source IDs.
c) Operator diagnostics endpoint (e.g. GET /api/diagnostics): provider/geocoder availability and latency only, no keys, no question text. Bounded retries only for transient errors.
d) Vendor Leaflet locally (or add SRI) and add a fallback message when tiles are offline.
e) Add run.bat that keeps the console open, prints the URL and the health endpoint, and shows startup errors.

PHASE 5: Retrieval quality
a) Add title/phrase weighting and a confidence threshold with an explicit no-result path.
b) Add optional hybrid search: a small multilingual ONNX embedding model (pinned URL + SHA-256, same pattern as the CLIP assets) fused with BM25 using reciprocal rank fusion. Must degrade gracefully to BM25 if the model is unavailable.
c) Add a grounding check: drop or flag generated claims not supported by retrieved text.
d) Create tests/eval_set.jsonl with 40 questions (English, Urdu, Roman Urdu, ambiguous, unanswerable) and an evaluation script reporting hit@3, no-answer accuracy, and location accuracy.

PHASE 6: User features
a) Nearby places (Haversine on places.json).
b) Live location: browser Geolocation (HTTPS/localhost, permission-based), distance to the selected place, and a "Directions" link (external Google Maps directions URL, no API key). Location is processed in the browser only unless the user explicitly requests a route.
c) "Copy coordinates" button, mobile-friendly larger map, zoom controls.
d) Trip planner: a structured itinerary (day, place, timing, source citation) generated only from corpus and places.json; refuse to invent opening hours or prices.
e) Link a confirmed image match to a map of the identified place (ask the user to confirm the landmark first).

